    "attack_surfaces": [
      {
        "function": "edit_cookie",
        "input": "cookie_index (scanf %llu unsigned)",
        "operation": "WRITE to msg[cookie_index] heap buffer with length msg_size[cookie_index]-1",
        "risk": "HIGH",
        "reason": "Attacker controls cookie_index (unsigned, only upper-bound checked < cookie_num). The index is used to read msg_size[idx] (int32 at 0x4160+idx*4) and msg[idx] (pointer at 0x4060+idx*8), then read() writes msg_size[idx]-1 bytes to the pointer at msg[idx]. If index can reach beyond the msg array (32 entries, 0x4060-0x415f), it could read msg_size from adjacent memory (e.g. msg_size[32]=0x41e0 area) and a pointer from msg[32] (0x41e0=urandom FILE*). The check is cookie_index < cookie_num, and cookie_num can be at most 32 (init sets 5, create adds up to 27 more for max 32). Index 0-31 are valid. No negative index possible with unsigned, but index values 0-31 are all within bounds. The key surface is: edit writes msg_size[idx]-1 bytes to heap buffer, where msg_size was set from user-controlled message_length in create_cookie (up to 0x100). If msg_size[idx] can be manipulated to be larger than the actual calloc size, heap overflow occurs."
      },
      {
        "function": "create_cookie",
        "input": "message_length (scanf %lld signed)",
        "operation": "calloc(message_length, 1) then read(0, buf, message_length)",
        "risk": "MEDIUM",
        "reason": "message_length is signed int64 from scanf, checked <= 0x100. If negative value passed (signed comparison with 0x100 via cmp rax,0x100; jle), negative values pass the check. calloc with negative size (interpreted as huge unsigned) would fail returning NULL, but read(0, NULL, negative_as_huge) could cause issues. Need S2 to verify if negative message_length bypasses the check and what happens with calloc/read."
      },
      {
        "function": "eat_cookie",
        "input": "random_index from /dev/urandom",
        "operation": "free(msg[random_index]) then swap msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[random_index]",
        "risk": "MEDIUM",
        "reason": "After free, the freed pointer's slot is overwritten with msg[cookie_num-1]. If random_index == cookie_num-1, the pointer is freed then immediately overwritten with itself (freed pointer written back). This is a potential UAF/double-free surface if the same slot can be accessed after free. The swap logic: msg[random_index] = msg[cookie_num-1]; if random_index == cookie_num-1, then msg[cookie_num-1] = msg[cookie_num-1] (self-assign after free). cookie_num-- makes this slot inaccessible via normal index, but the freed pointer may still be reachable if another reference exists."
      },
      {
        "function": "read_cookie",
        "input": "cookie_index (scanf %llu unsigned)",
        "operation": "printf('%s', msg[cookie_index])",
        "risk": "MEDIUM",
        "reason": "Attacker controls cookie_index (unsigned, < cookie_num check). printf uses %s format with msg[idx] as argument (not attacker-controlled format string). The string is printed until null terminator. If msg[idx] points to a buffer without null terminator (e.g. after edit fills entire msg_size[idx]-1 bytes without null), data beyond the buffer could be leaked. This is a potential info leak surface."
