source\":\"rdi parameter\"}],\"checks\":[],\"memory_operations\":[],\"calls\":[\"printf\",\"exit\"]}],\"attack_surfaces\":[{\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"input\":\"cookie_index (scanf %llu unsigned)\",\"operation\":\"WRITE to msg[cookie_index] heap buffer with length msg_size[cookie_index]-1\",\"risk\":\"HIGH\",\"reason\":\"Attacker controls cookie_index (unsigned, only upper-bound checked < cookie_num). The index is used to read msg_size[idx] (int32 at 0x4160+idx*4) and msg[idx] (pointer at 0x4060+idx*8), then read() writes msg_size[idx]-1 bytes to the pointer at msg[idx]. If index can reach beyond the msg array (32 entries, 0x4060-0x415f), it could read msg_size from adjacent memory (e.g. msg_size[32]=0x41e0 area) and a pointer from msg[32] (0x41e0=urandom FILE*). The check is cookie_index < cookie_num, and cookie_num can be at most 32 (init sets 5, create adds up to 27 more for max 32). Index 0-31 are valid. No negative index possible with unsigned, but index values 0-31 are all within bounds. The key surface is: edit writes msg_size[idx]-1 bytes to heap buffer, where msg_size was set from user-controlled message_length in create_cookie (up to 0x100). If msg_size[idx] can be manipulated to be larger than the actual calloc size, heap overflow occurs.\"},{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"input\":\"message_length (scanf %lld signed)\",\"operation\":\"calloc(message_length, 1) then read(0, buf, message_length)\",\"risk\":\"MEDIUM\",\"reason\":\"message_length is signed int64 from scanf, checked <= 0x100. If negative value passed (signed comparison with 0x100 via cmp rax,0x100; jle), negative values pass the check. calloc with negative size (interpreted as huge unsigned) would fail returning NULL, but read(0, NULL, negative_as_huge) could cause issues. Need S2 to verify if negative message_length bypasses the check and what happens with calloc/read.\"},{\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"input\":\"random_index from /dev/urandom\",\"operation\":\"free(msg[random_index]) then swap msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[random_index]\",\"risk\":\"MEDIUM\",\"reason\":\"After free, the freed pointer's slot is overwritten with msg[cookie_num-1]. If random_index == cookie_num-1, the pointer is freed then immediately overwritten with itself (freed pointer written back). This is a potential UAF/double-free surface if the same slot can be accessed after free. The swap logic: msg[random_index] = msg[cookie_num-1]; if random_index == cookie_num-1, then msg[cookie_num-1] = msg[cookie_num-1] (self-assign after free). cookie_num-- makes this slot inaccessible via normal index, but the freed pointer may still be reachable if another reference exists.\"},{\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"input\":\"cookie_index (scanf %llu unsigned)\",\"operation\":\"printf('%s', msg[cookie_index])\",\"risk\":\"MEDIUM\",\"reason\":\"Attacker controls cookie_index (unsigned, < cookie_num check). printf uses %s format with msg[idx] as argument (not attacker-controlled format string). The string is printed until null terminator. If msg[idx] points to a buffer without null terminator (e.g. after edit fills entire msg_size[idx]-1 bytes without null), data beyond the buffer could be leaked. This is a potential info leak surface.\"},{\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"input\":\"msg_size[cookie_index] controls read length\",\"operation\":\"read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1)\",\"risk\":\"HIGH\",\"reason\":\"The read length in edit_cookie is msg_size[idx]-1 (signed int, sign-extended via movsxd). msg_size is stored as int32. If msg_size[idx] is 0, then msg_size[idx]-1 = -1, sign-extended to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, causing read() to attempt reading a huge amount. If msg_size[idx] is negative (possible if create_cookie stores a negative message_length that passed the <=0x100 check), similar huge read. This could cause heap buffer overflow.\"}],\"facts\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Binary is PIE-enabled amd64 ELF, dynamically linked, not stripped, compiled with GCC 10.2.0\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F002\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Security: Full RELRO, NX enabled, Stack Canary, PIE enabled. No FORTIFY.\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F003\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"libc is glibc 2.39 (Ubuntu 2.39-0ubuntu8.8) at /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6\",\"source\":\"RUNTIME\"},{\"id\":\"F004\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"BSS layout: msg array at 0x4060 (32 char* pointers, 256 bytes), msg_size array at 0x4160 (32 int32 values, 128 bytes), urandom (FILE*) at 0x41e0, cookie_num (int64) at 0x41e8\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F005\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"init() pre-loads 5 fortune cookies (indices 0-4) via strdup, sets cookie_num=5, opens /dev/urandom, sets alarm(60)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F006\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie: checks cookie_num<=0x1f (31), checks message_length<=0x100 (signed comparison cmp rax,0x100; jle), calloc(message_length,1), read(0,buf,message_length), stores to msg[cookie_num] and msg_size[cookie_num], cookie_num++\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F007\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie: reads 1 byte from /dev/urandom, index=byte%cookie_num, printf msg[index], free(msg[index]), swaps msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[index] and msg_size[cookie_num-1] into msg_size[index], cookie_num--\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F008\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie: scanf(%llu) for index, checks index<cookie_num, read length = msg_size[idx]-1 (int32, sign-extended via movsxd), read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1)\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F009\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"read_cookie: scanf(%llu) for index, checks index<cookie_num, printf('%s', msg[idx]) - format string is fixed, not attacker-controlled\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F010\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie message_length check uses signed comparison (cmp rax,0x100; jle) with scanf %lld (signed). Negative values would pass the <=0x100 check.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F011\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie read length is msg_size[idx]-1 where msg_size is int32. If msg_size[idx]==0, length becomes -1 sign-extended to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF. msg_size is stored as int32 via 'mov ecx,edx' (truncation of int64 message_length to int32).\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F012\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie swap logic: if random_index == cookie_num-1, the freed pointer is written back to its own slot (msg[cookie_num-1] = msg[cookie_num-1] after free). cookie_num-- makes this slot inaccessible via normal indexing.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F013\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Runtime confirmed: menu displays 5 options, create asks 'How long is the message?' then 'Input your message:', edit asks 'Which cookie?[0-N]:' then 'New Message:', view asks 'Which cookie?[0-N]:'. Edit writes partial data over existing buffer (observed 'BBBB' + residual old data when edit length < original).\",\"source\":\"RUNTIME\"},{\"id\":\"F014\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"GOT is at 0x3f58 (0xa8 bytes, writable per section flags WA), but Full RELRO means GOT is made read-only after relocation at runtime\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F015\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg_size is stored as int32 (DWORD) at 0x4160, but message_length from scanf is int64. create_cookie truncates via 'mov ecx,edx' (stores lower 32 bits). msg_size[idx]-1 in edit_cookie uses 'sub eax,0x1' then 'movsxd rdx,eax' (sign-extend int32 to int64 for read length).\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F016\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie stores message_length to msg_size[cookie_num] as int32: 'mov ecx,edx' where edx=lower32 of message_length. If message_length is e.g. 0x100000001 (passes <=0x100 check? No - cmp rax,0x100 would fail). But negative like -1 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) passes <=0x100, truncated to int32 = 0xFFFFFFFF = -1 as int32, stored to msg_size. Then in edit, msg_size[idx]-1 = -1-1 = -2, sign-extended to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F017\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"calloc(message_length, 1) with negative message_length: calloc takes size_t (unsigned). If message_length=-1, calloc(0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, 1) will fail and return NULL. Then read(0, NULL, -1_as_size_t) would attempt to read huge bytes to address 0x0, causing SIGSEGV.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F018\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"urandom FILE* at 0x41e0 is at msg[32] position (0x4060 + 32*8 = 0x41e0). cookie_num at 0x41e8 is at msg[33] position. These are beyond the 32-entry msg array but within the msg_size array's address space (msg_size[32] at 0x4160+32*4=0x41e0 = urandom location).\",\"source\":\"ELF\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"id\":\"U001\",\"description\":\"Whether create_cookie's signed comparison (cmp rax,0x100; jle) allows negative message_length to bypass the size check and what the exact runtime behavior is (calloc failure, NULL deref, or exploitable condition)\",\"reason\":\"Static analysis shows signed comparison with negative-passing semantics, but runtime behavior of calloc with huge size and read to NULL needs dynamic verification\"},{\"id\":\"U002\",\"description\":\"Whether msg_size[idx]==0 or negative msg_size values can actually be achieved at runtime through the create_cookie path\",\"reason\":\"create_cookie truncates int64 message_length to int32 for msg_size. If message_length=0, calloc(0,1) returns valid pointer or NULL (implementation-defined), msg_size=0. edit would then read with length -1. Need S2 to verify if message_length=0 is accepted and what msg_size becomes.\"},{\"id\":\"U003\",\"description\":\"Whether the eat_cookie self-swap (random_index == cookie_num-1) creates a practically exploitable UAF condition\",\"reason\":\"The freed pointer is written back to its own slot, but cookie_num-- makes the slot inaccessible. Whether another reference to the freed chunk exists depends on heap layout and prior operations.\"},{\"id\":\"U004\",\"description\":\"CET (IBT/SHSTK) status cannot be determined from available tools\",\"reason\":\"checksec does not report CET flags; need to check NT_GNU_PROPERTY or runtime /proc/cpuinfo\"},{\"id\":\"U005\",\"description\":\"Whether read_cookie printf('%s') can leak heap data beyond the intended message when the buffer has no null terminator\",\"reason\":\"edit_cookie writes msg_size[idx]-1 bytes and null-terminates only if the last byte is 0x0a. If the read fills exactly msg_size[idx]-1 bytes without a trailing newline, no null is written, and the original buffer content beyond the write remains. printf %s would print until a null byte is found in the heap.\"}],\"raw\":{\"elf\":\"ELF64 PIE x86-64, dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, not stripped, GCC 10.2.0, source: fortunecookie.c, BuildID 84dab319bf6fe22134cf0338cbd8071fd8bc06c2\",\"sections\":\".text(0x1110,0x891,AX) .rodata(0x2000,0x266,A) .data(0x4000,0x10,WA) .bss(0x4020,0x1d0,WA) .got(0x3f58,0xa8,WA) .plt(0x1020,0xe0,AX) .init_array(0x3d58,8,WA) .fini_array(0x3d60,8,WA) .dynamic(0x3d68,0x1f0,WA)\",\"symbols\":\"Key functions: _abort(0x11f5) init(0x1232) create_cookie(0x1399) eat_cookie(0x14e1) edit_cookie(0x1628) read_cookie(0x1751) menu(0x17fd) main(0x187f). Globals: msg(0x4060,256B) msg_size(0x4160,128B) urandom(0x41e0,8B) cookie_num(0x41e8,8B) stdout(0x4020) stdin(0x4030) stderr(0x4040)\",\"disassembly\":\"main: switch(1-5) dispatching to eat/create/edit/read/exit. init: setvbuf x3, alarm(60), fopen(/dev/urandom), strdup x5 fortunes, cookie_num=5. create_cookie: check cookie_num<=31, scanf %lld message_length, check <=0x100(signed), calloc(len,1), read(0,buf,len), null-term if 0x0a, store to msg[cookie_num]/msg_size[cookie_num], cookie_num++. eat_cookie: fread 1B from urandom, idx=byte%cookie_num, printf msg[idx], free(msg[idx]), swap last into idx, cookie_num--. edit_cookie: scanf %llu idx, check idx<cookie_num, len=msg_size[idx]-1(movsxd), read(0,msg[idx],len), null-term if 0x0a. read_cookie: scanf %llu idx, check idx<cookie_num, printf('%s',msg[idx]).\"}},\"vulnerability_report\":{\"schema\":\"VulnerabilityReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"total_bugs\":2,\"high_severity\":2,\"medium_severity\":0,\"low_severity\":0},\"vulnerabilities\":[{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"title\":\"OOB_SIGNEDNESS_MISMATCH_IN_INDEX_CHECK\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie and read_cookie read the cookie index via scanf('%llu') (unsigned 64-bit), but the bounds check 'index < cookie_num' uses the 'jl' instruction (signed less-than comparison). A user-supplied unsigned value whose bit pattern represents a negative signed int64 (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF = -1) passes the signed 'jl' check because -1 < cookie_num (always positive). This allows negative indices to access memory before the msg[] and msg_size[] arrays in BSS, reaching FILE* pointers (stdout/stderr/stdin) and other global variables.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg (0x4060, char* array[32])\",\"msg_size (0x4160, int32 array[32])\",\"stdout (0x4020, FILE*)\",\"stderr (0x4040, FILE*)\",\"stdin (0x4030, FILE*)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"cookie_index via scanf('%llu') in edit_cookie and read_cookie\",\"entry_function\":\"main (menu option 3 or 4)\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker enters menu option 3 (edit) or 4 (view)\",\"scanf('%llu', &index) reads a large unsigned value (e.g., 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 = -8 signed)\",\"edit_cookie/read_cookie: cmp rdx, rax (index vs cookie_num); jl (signed) -> negative index passes check\",\"edit_cookie: msg_size[idx] loaded from 0x4160 + idx*4 (wraps to addresses before msg_size, e.g., 0x4140 for idx=-8)\",\"edit_cookie: msg[idx] loaded from 0x4060 + idx*8 (wraps to addresses before msg, e.g., 0x4020 for idx=-8 = stdout FILE*)\",\"edit_cookie: read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) writes attacker data to the pointer at msg[idx]\",\"read_cookie: printf('%s', msg[idx]) reads memory at the pointer stored at msg[idx]\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"Index should be validated as unsigned: 0 <= index < cookie_num, rejecting any value >= cookie_num including large unsigned values that are negative in signed representation.\",\"actual\":\"Index is read as unsigned (%llu) but compared with signed 'jl' instruction. Values like 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (=-1 signed) pass the < cookie_num check.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"The comparison instruction should be 'jb' (unsigned below) instead of 'jl' (signed less). Using 'jl' with an unsigned input creates a signedness mismatch that allows negative-index out-of-bounds access.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"OOB array access via negative index\",\"address\":\"edit_cookie: 0x1681 (cmp rdx,rax; jl), 0x16b6 (mov eax,[msg_size+idx*4]), 0x16d2 (mov rax,[msg+idx*8]), 0x16de (read); read_cookie: 0x17aa (cmp rdx,rax; jl), 0x17ce (mov rax,[msg+idx*8]), 0x17e1 (printf)\",\"expression\":\"edit_cookie: read(0, msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) with negative idx; read_cookie: printf('%s', msg[idx]) with negative idx\",\"target\":\"BSS memory before msg[] array: stdout FILE* (0x4020), stdin FILE* (0x4030), stderr FILE* (0x4040), BSS gap (0x4048-0x405f)\",\"size\":\"msg_size[idx]-1 bytes (edit), unbounded (read via printf %s)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In edit_cookie, a negative index causes msg[idx] to load a pointer from BSS memory before the msg array (e.g., idx=-8 reads stdout FILE* at 0x4020, idx=-4 reads stderr FILE* at 0x4040). read() then writes attacker-controlled data to that pointer's target, corrupting the FILE structure or other BSS data. The write size is msg_size[idx]-1, where msg_size[idx] is loaded from a corresponding negative offset in the msg_size array region.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In read_cookie, a negative index causes msg[idx] to load a pointer from BSS memory before the msg array (e.g., idx=-8 loads stdout FILE* at 0x4020). printf('%s', msg[idx]) then reads and prints memory at that pointer's address until a null byte is found, potentially leaking FILE structure contents or other sensitive data.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If a negative index resolves to a BSS location containing NULL (e.g., idx=-1 reads msg[0x4058] which is in the uninitialized BSS gap, likely 0x0), edit_cookie calls read(0, NULL, ...) and read_cookie calls printf('%s', NULL), both causing SIGSEGV.\"},{\"type\":\"DATA_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In edit_cookie with a negative index targeting a FILE* pointer (stdout/stderr/stdin), read() writes attacker data to the FILE structure, corrupting its internal state. This can affect subsequent I/O operations that use the corrupted FILE*.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1665, 0x1681, 0x1684\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie uses scanf with format string '%llu' (at 0x21c4, loaded via lea rdi,[rip+0xb58] at 0x1665) to read the index as unsigned, but the bounds check at 0x1681 (cmp rdx,rax) uses 'jl' (signed less-than, 0x1684) for the comparison index < cookie_num. A large unsigned value that is negative in signed representation passes this check.\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x178e, 0x17aa, 0x17ad\",\"description\":\"read_cookie uses the same scanf '%llu' format (at 0x21c4, loaded via lea rdi,[rip+0xa2f] at 0x178e) for unsigned index input, and the same 'jl' (signed less-than, 0x17ad) for the bounds check at 0x17aa (cmp rdx,rax). Same signedness mismatch as edit_cookie.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a7, 0x16b6, 0x16c3, 0x16d2\",\"description\":\"With a negative index, the array access 'lea rdx,[rax*4+0]' at 0x16a7 computes a negative offset for msg_size (base 0x4160), and 'lea rcx,[rax*8+0]' at 0x16c3 computes a negative offset for msg (base 0x4060). For idx=-8 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8): msg_size access = 0x4160 + (-8*4) = 0x4140 (within msg[] array area), msg access = 0x4060 + (-8*8) = 0x4020 (stdout FILE*). The loaded pointer is then used as the write target in read() at 0x16de.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie, read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4020-0x405f\",\"description\":\"BSS layout analysis: msg array starts at 0x4060. Negative indices access memory before msg: idx=-8 -> 0x4020 (stdout FILE*), idx=-4 -> 0x4040 (stderr FILE*), idx=-3 -> 0x4048 (BSS gap, likely 0). These FILE* pointers, when loaded as msg[idx], point to FILE structures in libc. edit_cookie writes to them (DATA_CORRUPTION), read_cookie reads from them (INFO_LEAK).\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm that scanf('%llu') with input 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 stores 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 in the local variable and that 'jl' treats it as -8 < cookie_num (passing the check)\",\"Verify that calloc/strdup-initialized msg entries at indices 0-4 do not interfere with the negative index computation\",\"Confirm the actual memory contents at BSS addresses 0x4020-0x405f (stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* values) to determine which negative indices yield valid pointers vs NULL\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-002\",\"title\":\"HEAP_BUFFER_OVERFLOW_VIA_MSG_SIZE_UNDERFLOW\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Integer Underflow\",\"description\":\"create_cookie accepts message_length=0 because the size check 'cmp rax, 0x100; jle' (signed comparison) passes for 0. This stores msg_size[idx]=0 (int32). In edit_cookie, the read length is computed as 'msg_size[idx] - 1' via 'sub eax, 0x1' (int32 subtraction), then sign-extended to int64 via 'movsxd rdx, eax'. When msg_size[idx]=0, the subtraction produces 0xFFFFFFFF (int32 = -1), which sign-extends to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (size_t = 18446744073709551615). This enormous size is passed to read(0, msg[idx], huge_size), where msg[idx] is a valid heap pointer from calloc(0,1), causing a massive heap buffer overflow.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"create_cookie\",\"edit_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg_size (0x4160, int32 array[32])\",\"msg (0x4060, char* array[32])\",\"heap chunk allocated by calloc(0,1)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"message_length=0 in create_cookie, then cookie_index in edit_cookie\",\"entry_function\":\"main (menu option 2 then option 3)\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker calls create_cookie (menu option 2) with message_length=0\",\"create_cookie: scanf('%lld') reads 0, cmp rax,0x100; jle -> 0 <= 0x100 passes\",\"create_cookie: calloc(0, 1) returns a valid minimum-size heap pointer (0x20 bytes in glibc)\",\"create_cookie: read(0, buf, 0) reads 0 bytes, returns 0\",\"create_cookie: msg_size[cookie_num] = 0 (stored as int32 via mov ecx,edx where edx=0)\",\"create_cookie: cookie_num++\",\"Attacker calls edit_cookie (menu option 3) with the index of the zero-size cookie\",\"edit_cookie: msg_size[idx] loaded as int32 = 0\",\"edit_cookie: sub eax, 0x1 -> eax = 0xFFFFFFFF (int32 = -1)\",\"edit_cookie: movsxd rdx, eax -> rdx = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (size_t)\",\"edit_cookie: read(0, msg[idx], 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) writes attacker data to heap starting at the 0x20-byte calloc(0,1) chunk, overflowing into adjacent heap memory\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"The read length in edit_cookie should be validated as non-negative and should not exceed the actual allocated buffer size. Additionally, create_cookie should reject message_length=0 or ensure msg_size is never 0.\",\"actual\":\"create_cookie checks message_length <= 0x100 (signed), allowing 0. edit_cookie computes read length as msg_size[idx]-1 without checking if msg_size[idx] is 0, causing integer underflow to -1 (0xFFFFFFFF int32), which sign-extends to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF for read().\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"No check for msg_size[idx] == 0 before computing msg_size[idx]-1 in edit_cookie. No lower bound check on message_length in create_cookie (should require message_length >= 1).\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"Heap buffer overflow via read() with underflowed size\",\"address\":\"edit_cookie: 0x16b6 (mov eax,[msg_size+idx*4]), 0x16b9 (sub eax,0x1), 0x16bc (movsxd rdx,eax), 0x16de (read)\",\"expression\":\"read(0, msg[idx], (int32)(msg_size[idx]) - 1) where msg_size[idx]=0 -> read(0, heap_ptr, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF)\",\"target\":\"Heap memory starting at the calloc(0,1) allocation (minimum 0x20 byte chunk), overflowing into adjacent heap chunks\",\"size\":\"Effectively unlimited (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF), kernel caps at MAX_RW_COUNT (~0x7FFFF000 = ~2GB), actual bytes read limited by pipe buffer (up to 65536 per call)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"read() writes attacker-controlled data starting at the calloc(0,1) heap chunk (0x20 bytes) and continuing into adjacent heap memory. The write size is effectively unlimited (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF before kernel capping), allowing corruption of heap metadata, adjacent heap objects, and any data stored in nearby heap chunks.\"},{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"The heap overflow corrupts heap metadata (chunk headers, free list pointers) and adjacent heap objects. glibc 2.39's tcache and fastbin structures may be corrupted, leading to heap state corruption that affects subsequent allocation/deallocation operations.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the overflow corrupts heap metadata (e.g., chunk size fields, free list pointers), subsequent heap operations (malloc/free/calloc) may trigger glibc heap consistency checks, causing abort() or SIGSEGV.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x13f2, 0x13f6, 0x13fc\",\"description\":\"create_cookie reads message_length via scanf('%lld') at 0x13ed, loads it at 0x13f2 (mov rax,[rbp-0x18]), then checks 'cmp rax, 0x100' at 0x13f6 with 'jle' (signed less-or-equal) at 0x13fc. Value 0 passes because 0 <= 0x100 (signed). No lower bound check exists.\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1416, 0x1498\",\"description\":\"calloc(message_length, 1) is called at 0x1416 with message_length=0. glibc calloc(0,1) returns a valid pointer to a minimum-size heap chunk (0x20 bytes). At 0x1498, 'mov ecx, edx' stores the lower 32 bits of message_length (0) to msg_size[cookie_num] as int32.\"},{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16b6, 0x16b9, 0x16bc, 0x16de\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie loads msg_size[idx] as int32 at 0x16b6 (mov eax, DWORD PTR [rdx+rax*1]), subtracts 1 at 0x16b9 (sub eax, 0x1), sign-extends to int64 at 0x16bc (movsxd rdx, eax), and passes as the count argument to read() at 0x16de. When msg_size[idx]=0: 0-1 = 0xFFFFFFFF (int32), sign-extended to 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (size_t).\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16d2, 0x16de\",\"description\":\"msg[idx] is loaded at 0x16d2 (mov rax, [rcx+rax*1]) as the buffer pointer for read(). Since create_cookie stored the valid calloc(0,1) return value to msg[idx], this is a valid heap pointer. read(0, valid_heap_ptr, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) writes attacker data to the heap starting at a 0x20-byte allocation, overflowing into adjacent heap memory.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm that calloc(0,1) in glibc 2.39 returns a valid non-NULL pointer (minimum 0x20 byte chunk)\",\"Verify the actual number of bytes read() returns when called with count=0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF from a pipe (expected: limited by pipe buffer size, up to 65536)\",\"Confirm that msg_size[idx] is indeed 0 after create_cookie with message_length=0 (no off-by-one or other adjustment)\"]}],\"non_vulnerabilities\":[{\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie frees msg[random_index] and swaps msg[cookie_num-1] into the freed slot, then decrements cookie_num. In the self-swap case (random_index == cookie_num-1), the freed pointer is written back to its own slot but cookie_num-- makes that slot inaccessible via normal indexing (index < cookie_num check fails). In the normal case, the freed pointer is replaced with a valid pointer from the last slot. No stale pointer remains reachable through edit_cookie or read_cookie.\",\"reason\":\"The swap logic correctly removes the freed pointer from all accessible array slots. cookie_num-- ensures the freed slot (now containing either a duplicate valid pointer or the freed pointer in the self-swap case) is beyond the accessible range. No use-after-free condition is created.\"},{\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"description\":\"read_cookie uses printf('%s', msg[idx]) with a fixed format string (not attacker-controlled). The format string is '%s' loaded from rodata at 0x21a9, not user input. No format string vulnerability exists.\",\"reason\":\"The format string argument to printf is a compile-time constant '%s' from .rodata, not derived from user input. The user only controls the index used to select which msg[idx] pointer to print, not the format string itself.\"},{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"description\":\"create_cookie's negative message_length path (e.g., -1) does not produce a useful heap overflow. calloc with a huge size (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) fails and returns NULL. read(0, NULL, huge) returns -1 (EFAULT) on Linux because the kernel's access_ok check fails for address 0 with huge count. msg[idx] is set to NULL, and subsequent edit/read operations on NULL cause crash, not controlled overflow.\",\"reason\":\"The negative message_length path leads to calloc failure (NULL return) and EFAULT from read(), not a controllable heap write. Only message_length=0 (BUG-002) produces a valid heap pointer with an underflowed size.\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether the negative index in edit_cookie/read_cookie can target useful non-NULL pointers beyond stdout/stdin/stderr (e.g., in the .data section at 0x4000-0x400f)\",\"reason\":\"The .data section (0x4000-0x400f, 16 bytes) contains initialized global data. Index values that map to 0x4000-0x400f would require idx = (0x4000 - 0x4060) / 8 = -12 for msg access. The contents of .data are not fully characterized.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Examine .data section contents to determine if any useful pointers or values exist at addresses reachable via negative indices.\"},{\"description\":\"Whether the heap overflow from BUG-002 can corrupt specific heap objects that affect program control flow (e.g., FILE structures allocated on the heap, or function pointers in heap objects)\",\"reason\":\"The heap layout depends on the sequence of calloc/strdup/free operations. The 5 initial strdup allocations and any user-created cookies determine which heap chunks are adjacent to the calloc(0,1) chunk.\",\"required_next_step\":\"Dynamic heap layout analysis to determine which heap objects are adjacent to the calloc(0,1) allocation and can be corrupted by the overflow.\"}]},\"primitive_reports\":[{\"schema\":\"PrimitiveReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"stage\":\"S3\",\"target_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"analyzed_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"total_primitives\":3,\"verified\":3,\"theoretical\":0,\"candidate\":0,\"unknown\":0},\"primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-001\",\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Signedness mismatch: edit_cookie and read_cookie read cookie index via scanf('%llu') (unsigned 64-bit), but bounds check 'index < cookie_num' uses 'jl' (signed less-than). Large unsigned values that are negative in signed representation (e.g., 2^64-11 = -11) pass the check, allowing negative-index OOB access to BSS memory before msg[] array.\",\"description\":\"read_cookie with idx=-11 loads msg[-11] = *(0x4060 + (-11)*8) = *(0x4008) = __dso_handle. __dso_handle is a self-referencing global pointer whose value equals its own address (PIE+0x4008). printf('%s', msg[-11]) prints the __dso_handle value as a string, leaking the PIE base address.\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"Signedness mismatch in index check (jl instead of jb)\",\"Negative index -11 passes bounds check\",\"read_cookie loads msg[-11] = *(0x4008) = __dso_handle (self-ref pointer)\",\"printf('%s', __dso_handle_value) prints PIE+0x4008 as string\",\"INFO_LEAK: PIE base address leaked\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"cookie_index via scanf('%llu') in read_cookie\",\"how\":\"Enter menu option 4 (view), then input 18446744073709551605 (2^64-11 = -11 signed). The signed 'jl' check treats -11 < cookie_num as true, allowing OOB access.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"Negative index must pass the signed 'jl' bounds check. idx=-11 (unsigned 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF5) is negative in signed representation, so -11 < cookie_num (always positive) passes.\",\"necessity\":\"Required - without bypassing the bounds check, no OOB access occurs.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"msg[-11] must resolve to a non-NULL pointer. idx=-11 maps to address 0x4060 + (-11)*8 = 0x4008, which is __dso_handle (a self-referencing pointer initialized by the loader).\",\"necessity\":\"Required - NULL pointer would cause SIGSEGV instead of info leak.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"__dso_handle must be a self-referencing pointer (value == own address). This is a property of the ELF loader initialization.\",\"necessity\":\"Required - the leaked value must be a meaningful address to compute PIE base.\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-11] = *(0x4060 + (-11)*8) = *(0x4008)\",\"alias\":\"__dso_handle global variable at link-time address 0x4008\",\"implication\":\"__dso_handle is a self-referencing pointer: its value equals its own address (PIE+0x4008). Leaking this value via printf('%s') reveals the PIE base address. GDB confirmed: x/gx 0x4008 shows 0x0000555555558008 (value == address).\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-11] = *(0x4160 + (-11)*4) = *(0x4134)\",\"alias\":\"High 4 bytes of msg[26] (pointer at 0x4130-0x4137)\",\"implication\":\"For read_cookie (INFO_LEAK), msg_size is not used. For edit_cookie (ARB_WRITE), msg_size[-11] determines the read length. After filling msg[5..26] with heap pointers, msg[26] high 4 bytes = 0x0000XXXX (small positive ~21845), making msg_size[-11]-1 = 21844 (valid read length).\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Run read_cookie with idx=-11 (input 18446744073709551605). GDB breakpoint at read_cookie+0x90 (printf call) to inspect rsi (the pointer being printed). Verify rsi points to __dso_handle and its value is a valid PIE address.\",\"environment\":\"Ubuntu, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled, amd64\",\"run_command\":\"python3 /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"GDB: rsi = 0x555555558008 at read_cookie+0x90 (printf call)\",\"GDB: x/gx 0x4008 = 0x0000555555558008 (self-referencing pointer)\",\"PoC: leaked PIE base = 0x5a65d0a98000 (varies per run due to ASLR)\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes the base address, but the INFO_LEAK primitive defeats it by leaking the __dso_handle value which encodes PIE+0x4008. Once PIE base is known, all BSS/data addresses become predictable.\"},{\"name\":\"ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"ASLR randomizes PIE base per execution, but the self-referencing __dso_handle pointer leaks the exact runtime PIE base, defeating ASLR for the binary.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"Bounds check: cmp rdx,rax; jl (signed less-than). scanf('%llu') reads unsigned index, but 'jl' treats it as signed. idx=-11 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF5) passes because -11 < cookie_num (signed).\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bf-0x17ce\",\"description\":\"Array access: lea rdx,[rax*8+0]; lea rax,[rip+0x2892] (msg base 0x4060); mov rax,[rdx+rax]. For idx=-11: 0x4060 + (-11)*8 = 0x4008 = __dso_handle address.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1 (printf call)\",\"description\":\"GDB breakpoint at read_cookie+0x90: rsi = 0x555555558008 (runtime __dso_handle address). x/gx 0x4008 = 0x0000555555558008 confirms self-referencing pointer (value == address). PIE base = 0x555555558008 - 0x4008 = 0x555555554000.\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"PoC execution: leaked __dso_handle value = 0x5a65d0a9c008, PIE base = 0x5a65d0a98000. Value verified via /proc/pid/mem read of address PIE+0x4008.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"0x5a65d0a9c008 (__dso_handle value = PIE+0x4008)\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"GDB at read_cookie+0x90: rsi=0x555555558008 (__dso_handle runtime addr), x/gx shows self-ref value 0x0000555555558008. PoC leaked PIE base=0x5a65d0a98000, verified via /proc/pid/mem. printf('%s', __dso_handle_value) prints the PIE address as a string until null byte.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"INFO_LEAK is DIRECT: the negative index directly selects __dso_handle, and printf directly prints its value. No intermediate object reuse or metadata manipulation needed. The leaked value is the __dso_handle self-referencing pointer (PIE+0x4008), from which PIE base is computed by subtracting 0x4008.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-002\",\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"DIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Same signedness mismatch as PRIM-001. read_cookie with idx=-8 loads msg[-8] = *(0x4060 + (-8)*8) = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* pointer.\",\"description\":\"read_cookie with idx=-8 loads the stdout FILE* pointer from BSS address 0x4020. printf('%s', stdout_FILE_ptr) reads and prints the contents of the stdout FILE structure in libc memory until a null byte is found, leaking libc internal data including the _flags field (0xfbad2887).\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"Signedness mismatch in index check (jl instead of jb)\",\"Negative index -8 passes bounds check\",\"read_cookie loads msg[-8] = *(0x4020) = stdout FILE* pointer\",\"printf('%s', stdout_FILE_ptr) reads FILE structure contents\",\"INFO_LEAK: libc FILE structure data leaked\"],\"controlled_element\":{\"what\":\"cookie_index via scanf('%llu') in read_cookie\",\"how\":\"Enter menu option 4 (view), then input 18446744073709551608 (2^64-8 = -8 signed). The signed 'jl' check treats -8 < cookie_num as true, allowing OOB access.\",\"reach_sensitive_operation\":true},\"preconditions\":[{\"id\":\"PC-01\",\"description\":\"Negative index must pass the signed 'jl' bounds check. idx=-8 passes because -8 < cookie_num (signed).\",\"necessity\":\"Required - without bypassing the bounds check, no OOB access occurs.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-02\",\"description\":\"msg[-8] must resolve to a non-NULL valid pointer. idx=-8 maps to address 0x4060 + (-8)*8 = 0x4020, which is the stdout FILE* global variable, initialized by setvbuf in init().\",\"necessity\":\"Required - NULL or invalid pointer would cause SIGSEGV instead of info leak.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-03\",\"description\":\"The stdout FILE structure must contain non-null bytes that printf can print. The _flags field (0xfbad2887) starts with 0x87 0x28 0xad 0xfb, all non-null.\",\"necessity\":\"Required - if the first byte were null, printf would print nothing.\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-8] = *(0x4060 + (-8)*8) = *(0x4020)\",\"alias\":\"stdout FILE* global variable at BSS address 0x4020\",\"implication\":\"stdout FILE* points to _IO_2_1_stdout_ in libc. printf('%s', stdout_ptr) reads the FILE structure contents, leaking libc data. GDB confirmed: rsi = 0x7ffff7fa75c0 = _IO_2_1_stdout_, first 8 bytes = 0x00000000fbad2887.\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"Run read_cookie with idx=-8 (input 18446744073709551608). GDB breakpoint at read_cookie+0x90 (printf call) to inspect rsi. Verify rsi points to _IO_2_1_stdout_ and leaked data contains the _flags field.\",\"environment\":\"Ubuntu, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled, amd64\",\"run_command\":\"python3 /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"GDB: rsi = 0x7ffff7fa75c0 (_IO_2_1_stdout_) at read_cookie+0x90\",\"GDB: x/8gx $rsi shows FILE structure contents starting with 0x00000000fbad2887\",\"PoC: leaked bytes = 8728adfb (first 4 bytes of _flags = 0xfbad2887 in little-endian)\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"ASLR\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"ASLR randomizes libc base, but the leaked FILE structure data (0xfbad2887 _flags) is a known libc constant that can be used to identify the libc version. The FILE structure also contains pointers that reveal the libc base.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17aa-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"Same signedness mismatch as PRIM-001: cmp rdx,rax; jl (signed). idx=-8 passes.\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bf-0x17ce\",\"description\":\"Array access for idx=-8: 0x4060 + (-8)*8 = 0x4020 = stdout FILE* global variable.\"},{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1 (printf call)\",\"description\":\"GDB breakpoint at read_cookie+0x90: rsi = 0x7ffff7fa75c0 = _IO_2_1_stdout_. x/8gx $rsi shows 0x00000000fbad2887 as first qword (FILE _flags field). This is non-attacker libc data.\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17e1\",\"description\":\"PoC execution: leaked 4 bytes = 8728adfb (hex) = 0xfbad2887 in little-endian = stdout _flags field. This is libc internal data, not attacker-provided.\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"\",\"read_value\":\"0xfbad2887 (stdout FILE _flags field, first 4 bytes leaked)\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"GDB at read_cookie+0x90: rsi=0x7ffff7fa75c0 (_IO_2_1_stdout_). x/8gx shows FILE structure starting with 0x00000000fbad2887. PoC leaked 4 bytes 8728adfb = 0xfbad2887 (stdout _flags). Non-attacker libc data confirmed leaked.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"INFO_LEAK is DIRECT: negative index directly selects stdout FILE*, printf directly reads FILE structure contents. The leaked _flags value 0xfbad2887 is a well-known glibc constant for stdout. The FILE structure also contains libc pointers that could be used to compute libc base (for S4).\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"PRIM-003\",\"type\":\"ARB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"VERIFIED\",\"kind\":\"INDIRECT\",\"source\":{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"root_cause\":\"Same signedness mismatch. edit_cookie with idx=-11 loads msg[-11] = __dso_handle (self-referencing pointer at 0x4008) and msg_size[-11] (aliased to msg[26] high 4 bytes). The self-referencing property of __dso_handle enables a two-step arbitrary write.\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie with idx=-11: Step 1 writes attacker-controlled address Y to __dso_handle (overwriting the self-reference). Step 2 uses the same idx=-11, but now msg[-11] = Y (the value written in step 1), so read() writes attacker data to *Y. This upgrades from RELATIVE_WRITE (fixed target = __dso_handle) to ARB_WRITE (target = any attacker-chosen Y).\"},\"chain\":[\"BUG-001\",\"Signedness mismatch: negative index -11 passes 'jl' check\",\"edit_cookie loads msg[-11] = __dso_handle (self-ref pointer: value == own address)\",\"msg_size[-11] aliases msg[26] high 4 bytes = 0x0000XXXX (small positive after filling msg[])\",\"Step 1: