,\"description\":\"22 cookies must be created (filling msg[5..26]) to make msg[26] a valid heap pointer. This makes msg_size[-11] = high 4 bytes of heap pointer = 0x0000XXXX (small positive).\",\"necessity\":\"Required - without filling msg[26], msg_size[-11] = 0 and read length = -1 (fails).\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-05\",\"description\":\"The target address Y written in step 1 must be a valid writable address. After step 1, the null-terminator check in edit_cookie dereferences msg[idx][read_return] = *(Y + read_return). If Y is invalid, SIGSEGV occurs.\",\"necessity\":\"Required for program survival after step 1. Y must point to writable memory.\",\"verified\":true},{\"id\":\"PC-06\",\"description\":\"The write value in step 2 must not cause the null-terminator check to crash. After read(), edit_cookie checks if the last byte written is 0x0a and replaces it with 0x00. This dereferences *(Y + read_return), which must be writable.\",\"necessity\":\"Required for program survival after step 2.\",\"verified\":true}],\"alias_key_findings\":[{\"expression\":\"msg[-11] = *(0x4060 + (-11)*8) = *(0x4008)\",\"alias\":\"__dso_handle global variable (self-referencing pointer)\",\"implication\":\"__dso_handle value == __dso_handle address (PIE+0x4008). This self-referencing property means: edit_cookie idx=-11 calls read(0, __dso_handle_addr, msg_size[-11]-1), which writes to __dso_handle itself. Step 1 overwrites __dso_handle with attacker-chosen Y. Step 2: msg[-11] = Y, read writes to *Y. GDB confirmed: x/gx 0x4008 = 0x0000555555558008 (value == address).\"},{\"expression\":\"msg_size[-11] = *(0x4160 + (-11)*4) = *(0x4134)\",\"alias\":\"High 4 bytes of msg[26] (8-byte pointer at 0x4130-0x4137)\",\"implication\":\"msg_size is int32 (4 bytes). msg_size[-11] at address 0x4134 overlaps the high 4 bytes of msg[26] at 0x4130. A 64-bit heap pointer like 0x0000555555XXXXXX has high 4 bytes = 0x00005555 (decimal 21845, small positive). So msg_size[-11] = 21845, and msg_size[-11]-1 = 21844 (valid read length). This is the key alias that enables the write: without it, msg_size[-11] = 0 and read length = -1 (fails). GDB confirmed: rdx = 0x5554 (21844) at edit_cookie+0xb6.\"},{\"expression\":\"Two-step write: Step 1 writes Y to __dso_handle, Step 2 writes data to *Y\",\"alias\":\"Self-referencing pointer enables write-what-where\",\"implication\":\"The self-referencing __dso_handle pointer is the critical structure. Step 1: read(0, PIE+0x4008, 21844) writes p64(Y) to __dso_handle. Now __dso_handle = Y. Step 2: read(0, Y, 21844) writes p64(marker) to *Y. Two different Y values (Y1=PIE+0x40b0, Y2=PIE+0x4100) both verified, proving ARB_WRITE (target address is attacker-controlled).\"}],\"verification_plan\":{\"method\":\"1. Leak PIE base via PRIM-001 (read_cookie idx=-11). 2. Create 22 cookies to fill msg[5..26] (enables msg_size[-11] alias). 3. Step 1: edit_cookie idx=-11, send p64(Y) to write Y to __dso_handle. 4. Step 2: edit_cookie idx=-11, send p64(marker) to write marker to *Y. 5. Verify via /proc/pid/mem that __dso_handle = Y after step 1 and *Y = marker after step 2. 6. Repeat with two different Y values to prove ARB.\",\"environment\":\"Ubuntu, glibc 2.39, PIE enabled, Full RELRO, NX, Canary, amd64\",\"run_command\":\"python3 /work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"expected_evidence\":[\"GDB: rsi=0x555555558008 (__dso_handle addr), rdx=0x5554 (21844=read length) at edit_cookie+0xb6\",\"Step 1: /proc/pid/mem shows __dso_handle = Y1 (e.g., 0x5a65d0a9c0b0)\",\"Step 2: /proc/pid/mem shows *Y1 = 0xDEADBEEFCAFEBABE\",\"Two-target test: Y1=PIE+0x40b0 wrote 0xDEADBEEFCAFEBABE, Y2=PIE+0x4100 wrote 0x4141414142424242\"]},\"mitigations\":[{\"name\":\"Full RELRO\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Full RELRO makes GOT read-only after relocation, preventing direct GOT overwrite. However, the ARB_WRITE primitive can write to any writable memory (BSS, heap, stack), so Full RELRO only blocks one specific target (GOT), not the primitive itself.\"},{\"name\":\"PIE\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"PIE randomizes addresses, but PRIM-001 (INFO_LEAK) leaks the PIE base first, making all BSS/data addresses predictable for the ARB_WRITE target selection.\"},{\"name\":\"NX\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"NX prevents executing shellcode on the stack/heap, but does not affect the ARB_WRITE primitive itself (which writes data, not code).\"},{\"name\":\"Canary\",\"present\":true,\"primitive_impact\":\"Stack canary protects against stack buffer overflows, but the ARB_WRITE primitive targets BSS/heap/global memory, not the stack. Canary does not affect this primitive.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684\",\"description\":\"Bounds check: cmp rdx,rax; jl (signed less-than). idx=-11 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF5) passes because -11 < cookie_num (signed).\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a7-0x16b6\",\"description\":\"msg_size access: lea rdx,[rax*4+0]; lea rax,[rip+0x2aaa] (msg_size base 0x4160); mov eax,[rdx+rax]. For idx=-11: 0x4160 + (-11)*4 = 0x4134 (high 4 bytes of msg[26]).\"},{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16c3-0x16d2\",\"description\":\"msg access: lea rcx,[rax*8+0]; lea rax,[rip+0x298e] (msg base 0x4060); mov rax,[rcx+rax]. For idx=-11: 0x4060 + (-11)*8 = 0x4008 = __dso_handle.\"},{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16de (read call)\",\"description\":\"GDB breakpoint at edit_cookie+0xb6 (read call): rsi=0x555555558008 (__dso_handle runtime address = write target), rdx=0x5554 (21844 = msg_size[-11]-1 = read length). Confirms: read writes to __dso_handle with valid small positive length.\"},{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4008 (__dso_handle)\",\"description\":\"Step 1 verification via /proc/pid/mem: after edit_cookie idx=-11 with p64(Y1), __dso_handle value changed from self-ref (0x5a65d0a9c008) to Y1 (0x5a65d0a9c0b0). Proves step 1 writes attacker-chosen address to __dso_handle.\"},{\"id\":\"E014\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"Y1=PIE+0x40b0\",\"description\":\"Step 2 verification via /proc/pid/mem: after second edit_cookie idx=-11 with p64(marker), *Y1 = 0xDEADBEEFCAFEBABE. Proves step 2 writes attacker data to the address specified in step 1.\"},{\"id\":\"E015\",\"type\":\"DYNAMIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"Y2=PIE+0x4100\",\"description\":\"Two-target ARB_WRITE proof: separate process with Y2=PIE+0x4100, marker=0x4141414142424242. /proc/pid/mem confirms *Y2 = 0x4141414142424242. Two different attacker-chosen addresses (Y1 and Y2) both written successfully, proving target address is attacker-controlled (ARB_WRITE, not RELATIVE_WRITE).\"}],\"verification_result\":{\"executed\":true,\"poc_path\":\"/work/test/poc-BUG-001.py\",\"written_value\":\"0xDEADBEEFCAFEBABE (at Y1=PIE+0x40b0) and 0x4141414142424242 (at Y2=PIE+0x4100)\",\"read_value\":\"\",\"fault_address\":\"\",\"return_code\":0,\"details\":\"GDB at edit_cookie+0xb6: rsi=0x555555558008 (__dso_handle addr), rdx=0x5554 (21844, valid read length from msg_size[-11] alias to msg[26] high 4 bytes). Two-step write: Step 1 writes Y to __dso_handle (self-ref pointer), Step 2 writes marker to *Y. Verified with two different Y values (Y1=PIE+0x40b0, Y2=PIE+0x4100) via /proc/pid/mem, both confirmed written. ARB_WRITE confirmed: target address is attacker-controlled.\"},\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"notes\":\"ARB_WRITE is INDIRECT: it requires two steps (step 1 overwrites __dso_handle self-reference with target Y, step 2 writes data to *Y). The intermediate step (modifying the self-referencing pointer) is what makes it indirect. Key alias: msg_size[-11] at 0x4134 overlaps msg[26] high 4 bytes. After filling msg[5..26] with heap pointers (0x0000XXXX_XXXXXXXX), high 4 bytes = 0x0000XXXX (small positive ~21845), giving read length 21844. Without this alias, msg_size[-11]=0 and read length=-1 (fails). The null-terminator check in edit_cookie (0x1705) dereferences *(Y + read_return), so Y must be a valid writable address for program survival. Full RELRO blocks GOT as a target but does not prevent the primitive itself.\"}],\"non_primitives\":[{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-001\",\"candidate_type\":\"CRASH\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"Negative indices that resolve to NULL or uninitialized BSS (e.g., idx=-1 to -7 mapping to 0x4058-0x4038 gap, or idx=-3 mapping to 0x4048 completed.0) cause SIGSEGV in edit_cookie (read to NULL) or read_cookie (printf NULL). This is a crash/DOS, not a useful primitive for exploitation. The crash at idx=-11 with invalid write data (non-address bytes) also occurs at edit_cookie+0xdd (0x1705) when the null-terminator check dereferences the overwritten pointer. These crashes are side effects of the OOB access, not independent primitives.\"},{\"primitive_id\":\"NP-002\",\"candidate_type\":\"RELATIVE_WRITE\",\"source_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"reason\":\"edit_cookie with idx=-8 (stdout FILE*) attempts read(0, stdout_FILE_ptr, msg_size[-8]-1). msg_size[-8] = *(0x4160 + (-8)*4) = *(0x4140) which is within msg[28] (uninitialized = 0). So msg_size[-8]-1 = -1 (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF), causing read() to fail with EFAULT (stdout FILE* + huge size overflows to kernel address space). Even after filling msg[28], msg_size[-8] aliases msg[28] low 4 bytes (large heap pointer value ~1.4GB), and stdout + 1.4GB overflows to kernel space. This path is a dead end for writing. The ARB_WRITE via idx=-11 (__dso_handle) is the viable write primitive.\"}],\"unresolved\":[]}],\"target_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"bug_reports\":[],\"impact_reports\":[],\"strategy_reports\":[],\"final_exploit_path\":\"\",\"final_report\":{}}",
